 <h2 id="check">Check your understanding</h2>
 <p>At least two questions for each outcome; Figure 2 covers outcome 6 too.</p>
 <section class="lesson quiz-lesson" data-lesson="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
  <h3 id="questions-title">Quiz: avoiding and defeating surface threats</h3>
  <p class="task" data-prompt aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons" data-choices></div>
  <p class="feedback" data-result role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
  <div class="buttons"><button type="button" data-new>Next question</button></div>
 </section>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 1</span> Your route passes a ridge 10 NM from a reported SAM radar. How can it help?</summary><p>Fly the leg with the ridge between you and the radar: direct terrain masking blocks its line of sight in that sector. Flying close to the ridge where the radar lights it too may also hide you from a pulse radar (indirect masking), but a pulse-Doppler radar may see through that.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 2</span> Why is jamming the missile of a command-guided SAM the wrong target?</summary><p>Its beacon is at the rear, facing the ground radar, and can be fairly powerful, so it is hard to jam. The missile is only as accurate as the ground radar's track, so jam, chaff or mask the tracking radar instead.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 3</span> DIRT, then MUD, then SINGER, all from the same SA-6. What has happened?</summary><p>Its radar went from searching, to tracking you, to a launch indication. The first needs awareness, the second a check that your plan keeps you out of its reach, the third a reaction now.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 4</span> At low altitude, below the minimum safe altitude, you get a launch indication with little time. What is the first option the F-16 handbook gives?</summary><p>Go lower and check away from the threat, using direct and indirect terrain masking to deny it acquisition. Climbing to the MSA first needs time between the indication and the engagement.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 5</span> Why not select afterburner to outrun an infrared missile you have just seen launched?</summary><p>Afterburner gives a signature that is hard for any flare to cover. Flare and turn to put it on the beam at once, with the least power that keeps your maneuvering speed.</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 6</span> Why does the time of flight of the rounds matter to a jink?</summary><p>The gun aims at where a steady aircraft would be when the rounds arrive. The longer they take, the further any change of heading, height or speed takes you from that point (Figure 2).</p></details>
 <details><summary><span class="outcome">Outcome 7</span> You are separated from your wingman after the attack. What now?</summary><p>Follow the egress plan with your own lookout to the briefed rendezvous point, and join with someone as soon as possible.</p></details>

 <h2 id="sources">Sources and applicability</h2>
 <h3>Military sources</h3>
 <p id="source-ewf"><strong>[1] US Air Force, Electronic Warfare Fundamentals, November 2000.</strong> A student text of Air Combat Command's Training Support Squadron, Det 8. <a href="https://falcon.blu3wolf.com/Docs/Electronic-Warfare-Fundamentals.pdf">Public copy</a>, read 13 November 2026 by printed page: 6-23 to 6-26 (radar horizon and terrain masking), 8-1 to 8-15 (missile guidance and AAA), 13-1 to 13-16 (chaff), 14-1, and 15-1 to 15-8 (infrared countermeasures).</p>
 <p id="source-mch"><strong>[2] US Air Force, MCH 11-F16 Volume 5, F-16 Combat Aircraft Fundamentals, 10 May 1996.</strong> <a href="https://falcon.blu3wolf.com/Docs/F-16%20COMBAT%20AIRCRAFT%20FUNDAMENTALS%20-%20Multi-Command%20Handbook%2011-F16%20Vol5.pdf">Public copy</a>, read 13 November 2026 by printed page: 125–127 (low and medium altitude), 146 (deliveries), 166–167 (recovery, curvilinear approach), 172–173 (pop-up), 202–203 (egress), 260 (vertical jinks, S-turns), 275–276 (terrain masking, threat reaction). Superseded; cited only for principles it states generally.</p>
